the pro-democracy opposition in Hong  Kong is celebrating a landslide victory  in local elections almost 3 million  people a record 71 percent of voters  went to the polls yesterday  pro-democracy candidates won 80 percent  of the contested district council seats  this comes after months of  anti-government protests ramie Inocencio  is in Hong Kong  ramie what's the mood there good morning  pro-democracy supporters and their  winning candidates cheered online and on  the streets  some people even popped champagne  bottles when one reviled pro-beijing  legislator named Junius ho lost his seat  other pro establishment candidates who  lost even publicly bowed while accepting  defeat and that here is a major cultural  act of apology now this all comes after  the past half year of often violent  protests at times those have crippled  the city and they've even damaged its  economy but demonstrators turned to  peaceful action on Sunday at the ballot  box looked past the months of chaos and  sent a very clear message they want  democracy now the record turnout says  they're angry at their government and by  extension Beijing and the Communist  Party and they're demanding the right to  have one person one vote to elect their  chief executive now they don't have that  now but the reality of these very local  elections is that they're high on  symbolism but very low on any political  leverage for any real kind of reform and  that means that the protests that we've  been seeing over the past six months  over this international very beautiful  and very well known city are probably  set to continue Jericka I'll take this  rainy inocencia forest in Hong Kong  ramie thank you very much
 

